{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his young age a regency was established with his mother Hodierna and his uncle Joscelin II acting as guardians. During this time the County of Tripoli faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal instability. Raymond III was known for his cautious nature and diplomatic skills which helped him navigate the turbulent politics of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem after he was named regent in 1174 following the death of King Baldwin III. He served as regent for the young Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was one of the most powerful nobles in the region and often clashed with other nobles especially Guy of Lusignan who was favored by the king’s mother Agnes of Courtenay. Raymond’s leadership was marked by efforts to maintain peace with Muslim neighbors and avoid unnecessary wars. He was instrumental in negotiating truces with Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. His cautious approach was often criticized by more aggressive knights who believed the Crusader states should take the offensive.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III’s relationship with Saladin was complex. Though he was a Christian noble he maintained a pragmatic alliance with the Muslim leader which allowed for periods of stability in the region. This alliance angered many of his fellow Crusaders who viewed it as betrayal. In 1187 Raymond joined the Christian army that marched to meet Saladin at the Battle of Hattin. He was one of the few nobles who survived the disastrous defeat. After the battle he fled to Tripoli and attempted to organize the defense of the remaining Crusader territories. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era of cautious diplomacy in the Crusader states. Raymond III was remembered as a skilled diplomat who sought peace in a time of relentless war. His legacy is one of pragmatism over zealotry in a world defined by religious conflict.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0001774868869688362}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -6.627816765103489e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.31327882409095764}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.001814506365917623}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2040065485052764e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "7", "logpro